  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The Strait of Hormuz remained closed in operational fact as of Day 222. Commercial traffic at the chokepoint collapsed to approximately four transits on October 4 — against a pre-war baseline of roughly 85 vessels per day — after the IRGC struck eight merchant vessels between September 28 and October 6, including a Panama-flagged tanker, a crude tanker off Oman, and a six-week-old Liberia-flagged LR2 disabled before completing its maiden voyage. Iranian authorities issued a formal statement on October 4 that the Strait would remain closed until Washington met a list of stated conditions, while war-risk insurance per supertanker passage held at approximately ten million dollars. Gulf oil flows outside Iran recovered to 81 percent of pre-war levels by September via pipeline and alternative routing, providing a partial pressure-valve that reduced the acute market-disruption premium without removing it. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>The first indirect contact between US and Iranian envoys since the collapse of the June Memorandum of Understanding occurred at the United Nations General Assembly in New York on approximately September 22-23. Special Envoy Steve Witkoff and Iranian representatives conducted three hours of shuttle diplomacy mediated by Qatari officials, with Witkoff confirming afterward that "mediators successfully completed a round of discussions" and that their work would continue. Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi conveyed Tehran's negotiating positions "explicitly and clearly" through Qatar before departing New York without a breakthrough. Iran proposed a phased seven-day Hormuz reopening contingent on the US meeting conditions from the expired June MoU; Trump publicly rejected the proposal during his UNGA address, in which he threatened to "annihilate the Islamic Republic" absent a peace deal. As of October 7, Araghchi awaited a formal US response via Qatari intermediaries; no response had been confirmed. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>The September 3-17 window that F3 required produced no qualifying indirect session. On September 7, the US envoy stated publicly there were "no plans" for a US-Iran meeting at the upcoming UN General Assembly, and on September 10 Trump said talks were "possible within days" — language that preceded rather than confirmed a session. Iran also made no public commitment to resuming mediated ceasefire talks before September 6 (the F2 horizon), despite Trump extending a limited ceasefire on September 5 and expressing an expectation that negotiations would resume. Tehran's posture in the first half of September was defined by the absence of formal diplomatic commitments on its side rather than by any affirmative engagement. The June MoU had formally expired in August 2026, and renewed limited US strikes around September 1-3 preceded a second quiet period. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>Israel's 25th Knesset voted to dissolve on July 17, 2026, setting October 27 elections, after Netanyahu's far-right coalition lost effective bloc cohesion on the Haredi military conscription dispute; polling in the weeks before dissolution placed the government bloc at 48 seats — 13 below the 61 needed for a majority. Four of five early-October polls give Eisenkot's Yashar party the largest-party position, typically at 22-24 seats against Likud's 18-21 in those same surveys, while one i24NEWS outlier gave Likud 28 to Yashar's 22. The overall bloc picture stands near 50-54 seats for the Netanyahu bloc versus 52-57 for the opposition, with Arab parties at 13 holding a decisive kingmaker role. The opposition bloc's declared boycott of Arab parties produces an asymmetry: Netanyahu's coalition has historically leveraged informal Arab support to reach the 61-seat threshold, and no such boycott constrains it on that flank. On October 7, the third anniversary of the 2023 Hamas attack, warning reports circulated and prediction markets moved, with PredictionHunt recording Yashar at 58-percent probability for the largest-party position. F7 resolved NO: the coalition held its one-seat majority (61 seats) through dissolution, and dissolution occurred at the natural end of the parliamentary term with an election date equal to — not earlier than — the forecast horizon. F5 updated to p=0.30 from 0.42. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>On Day 222, US bombers were removed from RAF Fairford following an Iranian-linked threat against the base, and no Iranian strike on a host nation was reported for a twenty-sixth consecutive day — a quiet period on the host-nation front after a string of IRGC-linked attacks in the summer. US military operations on Iranian territory have dialed back in favor of a maximum-pressure economic campaign: the US blockade on Iranian oil exports, combined with the self-imposed Hormuz closure, has squeezed Iranian hard-currency flows. Iranian officials publicly attributed damage to 111 petrochemical and pharmaceutical facilities from prior US strikes. Trump's October 2 statement that a decision on Iran is "imminent, the easy way or the hard way" and the September Camp David senior-official meeting on war strategy are consistent with a US posture that remains coercive in intent while operationally restrained. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:25%;">Escalation 25%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:55%;">Protracted 55%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:20%;">De-esc 20%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> IRGC tanker strikes, Trump's "annihilation" threat at UNGA, and hardliner operational autonomy maintain a meaningful escalation path absent a diplomatic breakthrough that F8 at p=17% does not yet support.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> Gulf oil rerouting at 81 percent of pre-war via non-Hormuz paths reduces the acute pressure that would force a deal, while the absence of a ceasefire framework (F8 p=17% inverse) and IRGC enforcement capacity sustain the current equilibrium through the near term.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> Anchored on F10 (p=20%) and F8 (p=17%), de-escalation requires the Qatar channel to produce a breakthrough that neither party has yet agreed to accept; a small probability of unilateral or partial Iranian Hormuz concession, decoupled from a formal ceasefire, accounts for the margin above F8.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · ~200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>The Islamic Republic reached Day 222 without a publicly active Supreme Leader. Ali Khamenei was killed on February 28, 2026, in the joint US-Israeli strike that triggered Operation Epic Fury; his son Mojtaba Khamenei, elected by the Assembly of Experts, formally assumed the Supreme Leadership on March 8, but has not appeared in public since that appointment, reportedly injured in the same strike that killed his father. A three-person transitional council comprising the sitting president, the judiciary chief, and a Guardian Council representative held formal authority during the interim. This leadership vacuum at the pinnacle of the velayat-e faqih system was the defining structural fact of Iran's domestic situation entering October 2026. Foreign Minister Araghchi and President Pezeshkian publicly represented Iran's diplomatic posture, with Araghchi at UNGA and Pezeshkian at the SCO summit in Kyrgyzstan on September 1 with Putin — a display of non-Western alignment. Parliament speaker statements on seven conditions for Hormuz reopening and IRGC operational tempo against tankers were consistent with the hardline security establishment retaining operational autonomy in the absence of visible Supreme Leader direction. Fuel and medicine shortages from US strikes on 60 petrochemical complexes and 44 pharmaceutical factories continued to generate domestic discontent. No elite fractures reached public visibility as of Day 222; the IRGC's sustained activity was consistent with institutional coherence at the operational level despite the political headlessness at the top. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

